  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 345 AM EDT MON AUG 24 2026\n\nAs the long term period opens, a high amplitude upper level ridge\nwill sit over the western CONUS, with troughing in the East.\nWithin this broader troughing, a relatively vertically stacked\nclosed low will be located over Ontario, on the eastern periphery\nof the upper level ridge. At the surface, a cold front will extend\nto the southwest of this low (roughly draped from the Upper\nMidwest into the Great Plains). As this low progresses to the\nsoutheast with time, the upper level trough axis will also\nprogress east. A broad area of height falls is expected across the\nOhio Valley, intensified by a shortwave that may approach the\nforecast area around Wednesday afternoon/evening. Concurrently,\nanother surface low originally over the Southern Plains will\ntranslate to the northeast, with a warm front lifting north toward\nTennessee. Although southwesterly winds should allow for very\nmodest moisture return for the forecast area on Wednesday, the\nbest chances for rain during the day will be closer to the\nvicinity of the front and the low, well southwest of the forecast\narea. Thus, the forecast area should remain relatively dry during\nthe day on Wednesday, with warmer weather than in previous days.\nHighs will rise into the mid to upper 80s, marking a return to\naverage to slightly above average temperatures, and with dewpoints\nrising back into the 60s, it will feel more humid, too. \n\nFollowing a relatively quiet day, rain chances will tick up again\nWednesday night into Thursday as the aforementioned Canadian low\ntranslates eastward and its cold front approaches the forecast\narea. Scattered showers and storms will be possible ahead of this\nboundary, although with shear remaining weak (with effective shear\nin the neighborhood of 20-25 kts), more robust storm organization\nwill be lacking. With the parent low so far north, frontal forcing\nwill also be relatively weak, with a barely perceptible\ntemperature and moisture gradient along the frontal boundary by\nthe time it approaches eastern Kentucky. These factors will keep\nconvective coverage scattered overnight Wednesday through\nThursday. \n\nBy Friday, the cold front will have cleared the area, marking a\nreturn to a drier pattern. The upper level trough axis will be\neast of the area; meanwhile, the upper level ridge will have\nexpanded eastward somewhat, with height rises across the forecast\narea and flow becoming increasingly zonal aloft as the trough\nlifts northward. Given this pattern, temperatures will likely be\nabove average through the duration of the period, with forecast\nhighs generally in the mid to upper 80s. An isolated shower or\nstorm will be possible in far eastern Kentucky on Friday afternoon\nwhere the ridging influence is the weakest; otherwise, rain\nchances will remain low for the duration of the period, with the\nmost notable weather being typical valley fog formation overnight.",
